  ],
  "title": "How to Plot and Read Linear Graphs",
  "content": "<p>A <strong>linear graph</strong> is a straight line given by \\(y = mx + c\\), where \\(m\\) is the <strong>gradient</strong> (steepness) and \\(c\\) is the <strong>y-intercept</strong> (where the line crosses the y-axis).</p><p>The gradient measures how much \\(y\\) changes for every 1-unit increase in \\(x\\):</p><p>$$m = \\frac{\\text{rise}}{\\text{run}} = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}$$</p><p>A positive gradient slopes upward left-to-right; negative slopes downward. To plot a line, substitute at least three \\(x\\) values, plot the points, and join with a ruler.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Plot \\(y = 2x - 1\\) for \\(x = 0\\) to \\(3\\).</strong></p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> \\(m = 2\\), \\(c = -1\\).</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> \\(x=0: y=-1\\); \\(x=1: y=1\\); \\(x=2: y=3\\); \\(x=3: y=5\\).</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Plot \\((0,-1)\\), \\((1,1)\\), \\((2,3)\\), \\((3,5)\\) and draw the line.</p>"
 },
